@h1 Citations & references h1@

This is the pattern Wit was built for. Instead of scattering page numbers and
publisher details through your sentences, you name each source once and then
refer to it in your prose as a single idea. The same definitions feed your
bibliography. Change a source's details in one place and every citation, plus the
whole reference list, updates with it.

@h2 The clutter problem h2@

Written out longhand, a citation swamps the sentence it belongs to — author,
title, year, publisher, page, every time. The reader loses the thread, and you
copy the same details from note to note, hoping they stay consistent. The fix is
to name the source and let a definition carry the details.

@h2 Name your sources h2@

Define each source once as a value — its bibliographic facts, defined a single
time:

@@@pre
#weil: Simone Weil, _Gravity and Grace_, 1952 !!
#berger: John Berger, _Ways of Seeing_, 1972 !!
pre@@@

@h2 Build an argument map h2@

Now name the _ideas_ you cite — a source plus a locator — so that in prose a
citation is two words long. A reference to a source, followed by the page, gives
a named idea:

@@@pre
#weil_attention: @weil p. 42 !!
#berger_looking: @berger p. 9 !!
pre@@@

@h2 Write prose that reads as ideas h2@

With the map in place, your sentences stay about the argument. A named idea
expands to its source's details and the locator you gave it:

@@@pre
Attention, as @weil_attention argued, is the rarest form of generosity.
pre@@@

Here is the whole pattern working live, from a definition to a woven citation:

#weil_demo: Simone Weil, _Gravity and Grace_, 1952 !!

#weil_attention_demo: @weil_demo p. 42 !!

Attention, as @weil_attention_demo argued, is the rarest form of generosity.

The citation reads as two words in your source and expands to the source plus the
page in the output — no publisher details cluttering the sentence.

@h2 Bare references when no locator is needed h2@

When a citation needs no page or locator at all, the bare source name is enough:

@@@pre
The lamp burned, as @weil knew it would.
pre@@@

@h2 A formatted bibliography from the same sources h2@

For a reference list — one entry per block, each laid out to a house style — a
definition can capture named fields and arrange them. You declare the fields
between double bars and drop each one in with a double-colon wrapper:

@@@pre
#ref ||author, title, year, page||
_::title::_, ::author:: (::year::), p. ::page::
ref#

@ref
  author: Trinity House
  title: Lighthouse Engineering — a Practical Guide
  year: 1981
  page: 203
ref@
pre@@@

The block-form definition with named captures reads best for a bibliography
entry, because each field is spelled out at the call. Any of the parameter forms
work against the same template — pick the one that reads clearest for your list.

@aside(class note) 💡 A note on delimiters, so a citation actually builds: in a
record argument, fields are written with a dash between key and value, as in
@@@code { author - Berger, title - Ways of Seeing } code@@@; in a form-fill body,
they are written with a colon, as in the reference call above. Mixing the two is
the most common reason a citation will not build. aside@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A comma inside a field value._ Commas separate fields, so a comma in a title
or publisher breaks the record. Keep values comma-free where the syntax splits on
commas. li@
@li _Using a capture in prose._ A double-colon capture only resolves inside a
definition body, not in your sentences. li@
@li _Mismatched field delimiters._ Dash for a record argument, colon for a
form-fill body (above). li@
ul@

